---
title: Carbonara
description: Lesson 4.4. The module together. Egg that thickens without scrambling, and fat and water that stay mixed.
servings: 2
time: 25 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, eggs-and-emulsions]
lesson: "4.4"
technique: thickening with egg
status: draft
---

> Technique: thickening a sauce with egg, off the heat. This lesson
> uses the module: egg proteins setting (4.1) and fat and water that
> must stay together (4.2 and 4.3). The amounts are per two people and
> scale in proportion.

Bring @water{2%litres} to a boil in a #large pot{} with @salt{10%g}. Cook @spaghetti{200%g} for the time on the packet, until just tender.

> Why so little water: the cooking water goes into the sauce, and less
> water means more pasta starch in each spoonful. You do not need a
> full pot to cook pasta.
-- source: Molly Birnbaum, "12 Common Cooking Myths, Debunked", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-05-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1706-12-common-cooking-myths-debunked ("You don't actually need a full pot")

While the pasta cooks, put @guanciale{100%g}(or pancetta, in small strips) in a cold #large frying pan{} over medium heat. Cook for ~{8%minutes}, until the fat has melted out and the pieces are crisp at the edges. Take the pan off the heat.

In a #bowl{}, whisk @egg yolks{3} and @egg{1} with @pecorino{50%g}(or parmesan, finely grated) and @black pepper{1%tsp}(freshly ground) into a thick paste.

Scoop out a cupful of the pasta water, then drain the pasta. Add the pasta to the frying pan, off the heat, and toss it in the fat.

Add the egg mixture and 4 tablespoons of the pasta water. Toss without stopping for ~{1%minute}, until the sauce thickens and coats the pasta. If it is stiff, add more pasta water a spoonful at a time.

> Why off the heat: egg begins to set at around 60 °C and its main
> protein sets at about 75 to 84 °C. A pan on a flame is far above
> both. The heat left in the pasta and the pan is enough to thicken
> the egg and not enough to scramble it.
-- source: Wang et al., "The Thermodynamic and Gelation Properties of Ovalbumin and Lysozyme", Gels 11(6):470, 2025, https://doi.org/10.3390/gels11060470 ; Mannott, Bosse and Gibis, "Recent Advances in Egg White Biotechnology, Microbiology, and Quality", Comprehensive Reviews in Food Science and Food Safety 25:e70495, 2026, https://doi.org/10.1111/1541-4337.70495 (Table 1)

> Science: cheese is the other thing that can go wrong. In a physics
> study of cacio e pepe, a close relative of this sauce, cheese in hot
> water with no starch formed large clumps at about 65 °C. With starch
> at more than 1% of the cheese's weight the sauce stayed smooth over a
> wider range of temperatures; below that it collapsed into what the
> authors named the Mozzarella Phase. Look up: protein aggregation,
> phase separation, starch.
-- source: Bartolucci et al., "Phase behavior of Cacio e Pepe sauce", Physics of Fluids 37(4):044122, 2025, https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00536 (the study is of cacio e pepe, not carbonara; it does not measure the starch in pasta water, and recommends weighed starch because that amount is uncontrolled)

> Safety: the egg in this sauce is thickened, not cooked firm, which is
> below official advice. The US FDA says to cook eggs until yolk and
> white are firm, and to use pasteurised eggs for dishes where they are
> not. This matters most for children, older adults, pregnant women
> and people with weakened immune systems.
-- source: US Food and Drug Administration, "What You Need to Know About Egg Safety", content current as of 2024-03-05, https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/what-you-need-know-about-egg-safety

> Next time, without the recipe: for two, 200 g of pasta, 3 yolks and
> 1 whole egg, 50 g of hard cheese and 100 g of cured pork; scale it
> all together. Everything meets off the heat, with pasta water to
> loosen it.
